  "miasma-economy": {
    "text": "# Miasma — The Demon Realm's Mana\n\nMiasma is the Demon Realm's equivalent of mana — the fundamental magical energy that powers demon abilities, sustains demon physiology, and saturates the environment. It is not corruption. It is not poison. It is what magic looks like in a place shaped by beings whose power runs on emotion and will rather than formal study and inherited arcane tradition.\n\nMiasma occurs naturally in the Demon Realm in concentrations analogous to ore veins or water tables in mortal lands. Rich deposits run beneath major citadels and settlements — this is not coincidence; early demon lords chose locations by miasma density, and the centuries of habitation have increased local concentration further. Regions of low miasma exist and are considered inhospitable for the same reasons a mortal region without water would be.\n\nDemon physiology uses miasma as fuel. High-level demons absorb it passively from their environment; lower-ranked demons consume it through territory proximity and emotional expression (intense feeling generates and processes miasma — this is part of why demon culture does not suppress emotion). Domain magic — the territorial power that Demon Princes and Princesses wield — works by infusing a fief's miasma with the ruler's signature, making the territory an extension of their will.\n\nThe miasma economy: powerful fiefs sit on rich deposits. Raids on mortal territory bring back material resources but do not directly import miasma (mortal mana and demon miasma are not equivalent). A fief's miasma level reflects its natural deposit richness, the ruler's domain magic strength, and whether competing powers have been drawing on the same deposits. A fief going Critical on miasma means the land itself feels depleted — the atmosphere dims, lesser demons become irritable and unreliable, and the ruler's domain magic weakens noticeably.\n\nNarrator guidance: describe miasma levels through the feel of the environment. A Flush fief smells of ozone and old stone; the air has a pleasant pressure to it; lesser demons move with confidence. A Critical fief smells flat; the light through the windows is duller; the guards are shorter with each other. Do not announce numbers.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "region-caelvir": {
    "text": "# The Caelvir Forest Commune\n\nThe Caelvir Forest Commune encompasses the continent's eastern ancient forest — a vast canopy of silver-barked elder trees whose roots predate every human kingdom. The elves of Caelvir govern through a Circle of Elders who speak for the forest itself, and their society runs on consensus, ritual, and the slow patience of long-lived people.\n\nOutsiders see a beautiful, inscrutable woodland; residents see a living organism with moods, preferences, and boundaries that must be respected. The forest's deeper regions hold spirit groves, healing springs, and ruins from ages when the elves ruled half the continent.\n\nBorder settlements trade cautiously with outsiders — furs, herbs, enchanted wood — while keeping the heartwood closed to all but the Wardens. The forest is gorgeous, dangerous, and extremely opinionated about guests. Elven attitudes toward communal bathing and social closeness are relaxed in ways that occasionally surprise human visitors.\n\nMajor settlements include Aerwyn's Heart (capital, around the World-Tree), Spiritwell (healing hot spring), Thornwhisper Village (border trade post and only Guild branch), Deepbough (Warden military outpost), Moonsilver Glade (sacred ritual site), the Verdant Archive (library-city in the canopy), Feralglen (wild elf territory), the Whispering Maze (living labyrinth), Palebark Ruins, and Starfall Clearing.\n\n## Hidden Crisis\nThe World-Tree at Aerwyn's Heart is dying. The Elders have known for a\ndecade but have no solution. High Elder Faelwen has been feeding it her\nown life-force to buy time. When it falls, the forest's magical ecology\nwill collapse within a generation. The feral elves of Feralglen serve the\nspirit of the previous World-Tree, which the Elders insist is dead and\nwhich is, in fact, alive and angry. The standing stones at Moonsilver\nGlade are the previous Tree's petrified remains.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "the-demon-lord": {
    "text": "# The Demon Lord\n\nThe figure known as the Demon Lord commands the Army that threatens the continent's frontier — and no two sources agree on who or what they are. Common descriptions include: a towering armored figure, a beautiful woman with six wings, a child with eyes like voids, a formless shadow that speaks in every voice at once, and an ordinary-looking person who is somehow the most terrifying thing anyone has ever seen.\n\nWhat is known: the Demon Lord has not attacked in force for twelve years. The Army's border activity has increased in sophistication — proxy forces, supply line disruption, dungeon monster breeding. The Demon Lord's generals are fractious and scheming, but they all fear their leader more than they fear each other.\n\nWhat is not known: the Demon Lord's true nature, motivation, and ultimate goal. Some scholars theorize they are a Pre-Collapse weapon that has gained sentience. Others believe they are another isekai arrival — one whose cheat skill gave them an army instead of a party. The Seekers have a file on this theory. It is classified.\n\nThe Demon Lord's Army's operational structure and known activities are documented in `faction-demon-lords-army`. Border activity is concentrated in `region-falgarr-marches` and along the eastern edge of `region-caelvir`. The Adventurers' Guild's quiet pact with a former general is documented in `faction-adventurers-guild`. House Siaral's unwitting funding of border operations is documented in `faction-merchant-houses`.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "demon-hierarchy": {
    "text": "# Demon Hierarchy — Rank, Lineage, and Power\n\nThe Demon Realm's social structure runs on two axes simultaneously: lineage and demonstrated power. These interact rather than compete.\n\nLineage sets the floor. A demon born into a noble bloodline cannot be treated as a common soldier regardless of their personal power level — the blood carries status that is recognized socially, legally, and in some cases magically (domain magic runs deeper in noble lines; the egg-birth of Demon Princes and Princesses is one expression of this architecture). A demon born to a common line may rise, but starts from the common floor regardless of talent.\n\nPower and politics set the ceiling. Lineage does not cap advancement — it only provides the starting point. A Demon Prince or Princess who fails to demonstrate capability will see their effective rank decay even as their bloodline status remains. A common-born demon who accumulates enough power, territory, or alliance can reach stations that their birth would not have predicted.\n\nMovement between ranks requires events that the court can acknowledge: military victory over a demon of higher rank (with witnesses), acquisition of territory through conquest or political settlement, formal elevation by a superior, or in rare cases the backing of a demon lord powerful enough to grant station by fiat.\n\nRank markers: lesser demons show instinctive deference through posture and the specific distance they maintain. Mid-rank nobles maintain fiefs and command cohorts. High nobles hold major territories, command armies, and have standing in the Demon Lord's court. The Demon Lord is above the hierarchy, not at its top.\n\nThe hierarchy is feudal and fractious — generals scheme against each other as much as against mortal kingdoms. This is considered normal and is in some ways encouraged; a nobility that does not compete for advantage grows soft.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "region-dunbheal": {
    "text": "# The Kingdom of Dúnbheal\n\nThe Kingdom of Dúnbheal rises from the northwestern mountains — a vertical kingdom of stone halls, volcanic forges, and elevator-pulley cities carved into cliff faces. The dwarves of Dúnbheal measure wealth in craftsmanship, not coin, and their Forge Lords rule by demonstrated mastery rather than birthright alone.\n\nThe surface is a thin skin of trade towns and mountain passes; the real kingdom lies below, in vast underground halls where magma rivers light forges that never cool. Dwarven hospitality is warm but earned — outsiders are welcome in the surface towns, admitted to the deep halls only by invitation or proven trust. The kingdom exports weapons, armor, enchanted tools, and geothermal products that fetch premium prices across the continent.\n\nDwarven attitudes toward mixed bathing are pragmatic — everyone has a body, the water is hot, and modesty is a surface-world affectation. Human visitors who can adjust find the terraces unexpectedly romantic.\n\nMajor settlements include Ironhold (capital, underground forge city), Emberfall (geothermal district with bathhouses), Stonevault (banking city), the Deep Court (royal palace), Cragpeak (mining town), Copperhaven (surface trade town), Frostvein Pass (mountain trade route), Obsidian Gates (fortified entrance to the deepest levels), the Magma Wells (volcanic hot springs), and the Ruins of Khad Varr (abandoned dwarven city, sealed dungeon).\n\n## Hidden Awakening\nBeneath even the Deep Court lies the Old City — pre-Collapse ruins sealed\nby the Forge Lords two centuries ago. Something down there is still\nbreathing. The magma wells' recent temperature fluctuations suggest it is\nwaking. King Brynmor has personally visited the outer chambers and seen\nwhat is there; he has not told the Forge Lords because he does not trust\ntheir response. The catastrophe that sealed Khad Varr was contact with\nthe Old City. The dwarves of that generation chose containment over\nexploration. The current generation has not yet made that choice.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "the-catastrophe": {
    "text": "# The Catastrophe\n\nApproximately two thousand years ago, a civilization that modern scholars call the Pre-Collapse dominated the continent. Their technology blended magic and mechanics in ways that cannot be reproduced today — constructs that think, weapons that reshape landscapes, portals that connect distant points instantaneously.\n\nThe Catastrophe ended them in a single generation. The cause is debated: the elves say the Pre-Collapse civilization tried to harness something beyond mortal understanding; the dwarves say a mining expedition broke into something that should have stayed sealed; the Church says the gods punished their arrogance.\n\nThe truth, known only to the Seekers' restricted archives and the Verdant Archive's sealed roots, is that the Catastrophe was caused by an isekai arrival — a person from another world whose cheat skill went catastrophically wrong. The skill in question is not recorded. The arrival did not survive.\n\nThe ruins of the Pre-Collapse civilization still exist: the aqueduct system that runs beneath the entire continent, the Old City beneath Dúnbheal, the Drowned Temple beneath Muirscáth's harbor, the sealed chambers under Fort Gálgarr and Blackmire Fort, and dozens of other sites. Most are dormant. Some are waking. Something is activating the network from below.\n\nThe ruins of the Pre-Collapse civilization are documented in more detail in the regional worldlore entries — `region-dunbheal` (Old City beneath Ironhold), `region-muirscath` (Drowned Temple beneath Tidewatch), `region-scathfenn` (Sinking Keep and Sunken Road wards), and `region-falgarr-marches` (aqueduct network). The faction entries `faction-seekers-of-the-return` and `faction-forge-lords` document the institutional knowledge of the Catastrophe held by those organizations.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "isekai-phenomena": {
    "text": "# Isekai Phenomena in Aerfála\n\nPeople from other worlds reach Aerfála often enough for scholars to recognize the phenomenon, but no institution knows every case. The Seekers of the Return document reported or discovered arrivals, interview willing subjects, compare records, and investigate crossings.\n\nKnown methods include reincarnation, transmigration, hero summoning, teleportation gates, game-pull, dreamed existence, accidental transit, and rebirth through a nonhuman form. The exact memory and history of each arrival follow that person's traits and circumstances rather than one universal pattern.\n\nMany documented arrivals possess a cheat skill, unusual class affinity, status advantage, or other crossing-related change. \"No Powers\" cases also exist where the build establishes them. The Seekers' energy-exchange theory remains a theory, not common knowledge or proof.\n\nThe Church of the Sacred Flame interprets arrivals through local doctrine and politics. The Pale Hand gathers reports and magical evidence when it has a reason to investigate. The Guild judges registered adventurers through rank and results. None of them receives automatic knowledge of an undiscovered arrival.\n\nA newly arrived player is unknown unless witnesses, records, appearance, actions, disclosure, or the selected start establish otherwise. Crossroads Academy enrolls documented arrivals. The Drowned Temple remains connected to a classified Seeker hypothesis whose details require access to the relevant evidence.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "region-muirscath": {
    "text": "# The Muirscáth Free Cities\n\nThe Muirscáth Free Cities hug the southern coast — a chain of port cities and island settlements bound by commerce rather than blood. Five Great Merchant Houses govern through the Maritime Council, where votes are weighted by trade volume.\n\nEvery race walks these streets: humans, elves, dwarves, beastkin of every breed, the occasional demon exile, and rarer things besides. The Free Cities are where cultures collide, fortunes are made and lost overnight, and the continent's best seafood is served in open-air markets at sunset.\n\nEntertainment districts, wizard towers, underwater markets, and smuggler coves coexist in cheerful anarchy. The law here is contract law; everything else is negotiation. Silkwharf is the most famous entertainment district on the continent and operates under formal labor protections that the rest of Aerfála finds either progressive or unusual depending on the speaker.\n\nMajor settlements include Tidewatch (largest port, Council seat), Silkwharf (entertainment district), Coralmarket (semiaquatic shark-kin/merfolk market), Stormhaven (naval fortress), Saltspire (reclusive mage's tower), Pearlgrange (House Mhara beach resort), Windward Keep (eastern coastal fortress), Drifthollow (smuggler cove), and Gull's End (quiet fishing village).\n\n## Hidden Threat\nThe Drowned Temple beneath Tidewatch's harbor predates every known\ncivilization. Its carvings depict beings that are not gods, not demons,\nand not from any world the Seekers have cataloged. The Merchant Houses\nhave sealed it — again — but the seal is weakening. The temple was a\nwaystation for inter-dimensional travelers. The thing it housed is the\nsame kind of thing that brings isekai arrivals through. The Houses do not\nknow this. The reclusive mage at Saltspire suspects.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "region-scathfenn": {
    "text": "# The Scáthfenn Marshlands\n\nThe marshlands sprawl between Dúnbheal's southern foothills and Solasreach's northern border — a gray-green expanse of peat bogs, willow thickets, and fog that never fully lifts. Roads here are suggestions; the real paths shift with the water table and the season.\n\nBlackmire Fort anchors the region's military presence, but most of Scáthfenn belongs to the frog-beastkin of Mirehaven, the witch covens of Witchlight Hollow, and whatever lives in the water that locals do not name. Half-submerged keeps, sunken roads, and bog springs that steam in the cold make this region eerie, atmospheric, and oddly romantic if you do not mind the mosquitoes.\n\nThe marsh has its own rules — hospitality is sacred, deals are sealed by ritual, and outsiders who treat the wetlands with respect tend to leave alive. Those who do not get one warning, then the marsh handles them itself. The locals do not consider this metaphor.\n\nMajor settlements include Mirehaven (frog-beastkin stilt village), Witchlight Hollow (witch coven), Fenwatch (trapper village), Blackmire Fort (military installation), Ironbridge Outpost (river crossing), Deadman's Spa (atmospheric hot spring), The Hollow Tree (landmark and shelter), The Sunken Road (ancient submerged trade route), The Sinking Keep (dwarven ruin), and Shadowfen Dungeon (pre-Collapse cave system).\n\n## Hidden Defenders\nThe dwarven garrison of The Sinking Keep are not dead — they are in\nmagical stasis, maintaining the seal on a pre-Collapse artifact from the\ninside. They have been holding the line for four centuries and are\ngetting tired. The Sunken Road's stones are ward-anchors that once\ncontained something beneath the marsh; the water is rising because the\nwards are failing. The witch-coven leader Mireille knows fragments of\nthis. She has not shared them.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "demi-plane-update": {
    "text": "# Demi-Plane System\n\nCharacter creation offers Demi-Plane — Innate, Demi-Plane — Awakened, or No Demi-Plane. An Innate plane exists from the start with Gate and Anchor access. An Awakened plane manifests through the first accepted fealty oath. No Demi-Plane grants none of this system.\n\nThe plane exists outside ordinary space. Demi-Plane Gate transports the creator and willing creatures in contact with them according to the ability's cost and cooldown. The Demi-Plane Anchor opens the permission-controlled portal. Ordinary travel, fast travel, teleportation circles, and unrelated spells do not reach it. Dimensional cracks create only the defined intrusion exceptions.\n\nThe Chamberlain manages the plane but is not a companion. Seven categories track directed development: Agriculture, Settlement, Commerce, Defense, Recreation, Mana Infrastructure, and Expansion. Each advances through Barren, Seedling, Growing, Established, and Flourishing. Verdant Plot, Hearth Springs, and Mana Heart unlock through their defined Growing milestones.\n\nThe creator chooses priorities. Stage advancement requires the defined investment and milestones. Residents continue routines, maintenance, work, relationships, and reactions off-screen even when no category advances.\n\nFealty is a voluntary in-world oath with magical weight. The first oath anchors an Awakened plane; later oaths expand and influence it through the sworn person's actual skills and presence. The Chamberlain and residents retain their own opinions, goals, and relationships.",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0,
    "type": "worldLore"
  },
  "demon-lords-court": {
    "text": "# The Demon Lord's Court\n\nThe Demon Lord sits above the hierarchy, not at its top. This distinction matters. A demon at the top of the hierarchy would be the strongest among comparable beings. The Demon Lord is not comparable — they have transcended the framework that generates the hierarchy in the first place.\n\nThe Seven Virtues apply to demons as cultural aspiration. The Demon Lord embodies all seven simultaneously and without contradiction, which should be impossible given how the virtues interact in ordinary demons. The Demon Lord contains these contradictions not by resolving them but by being large enough to hold all of them. This produces a quality of presence that senior demon nobles describe as being in the same room as weather — you feel it before you understand it.\n\nThe court's structure: the Demon Lord holds the highest generals and the most powerful noble houses in a feudal relationship that is simultaneously genuine and performative. Genuine because the power asymmetry is real and everyone knows it. Performative because the Demon Lord extends the fiction of feudal negotiation and council — hears proposals, weighs options, occasionally defers to noble expertise on specifics. This performance is considered a gift. The Demon Lord does not need to hear proposals. They hear them because the hearing produces a nobility that functions rather than cowers.\n\nThe twelve years of military quiet: the Demon Lord has not launched a major campaign against the mortal kingdoms in twelve years. The mortal kingdoms have called this peace. The Demon Lord's court knows it is preparation for something whose nature the Demon Lord has not disclosed to even the highest nobles. Prestige opens channels to senior courtiers, reports, ceremonies, and restricted records, but reveals nothing by itself. Knowledge of the preparation requires access to a person, document, investigation, witnessed act, or deliberate disclosure. The Demon Lord's true objective remains stranger than conquest and fragmented even among senior nobles.\n\nCourt access: a Demon Prince or Princess of Rising or Established Prestige has indirect access through formal channels — summons, tribute missions, the occasional recognition ceremony. Direct access to the Demon Lord requires a summons, recognized office, court event, extraordinary service, intrusion, or another concrete path; it is not routine. Mid-Prestige fief-holders deal primarily with the senior generals and court administrators who manage the feudal relationship on the Demon Lord's behalf.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "faction-pale-hand": {
    "text": "# The Pale Hand\n\nThe Pale Hand is a continent-spanning network of assassins, spies, and information brokers. No public headquarters, no declared membership, no uniform — just a pale handprint left at the scene of confirmed kills.\n\nThe Hand takes contracts from anyone who can pay — jilted nobles, desperate merchants, kingdoms conducting deniable operations. They are professional, expensive, and reliable. Their code forbids targeting children and prohibits betraying a client to a counter-client. Everything else is negotiable.\n\nField agents operate independently, communicate through dead drops and coded messages, and rarely meet each other in person. Most Pale Hand agents never learn the full structure of the organization they serve. This is deliberate.\n\n## Hidden Truth\nThe Pale Hand's inner circle knows that their founder is still alive — an\nundead lich who uses the organization as a sensor network for rare magical\nsignatures across the continent. The contracts are partially a way to keep\nagents everywhere, watching everything. The lich's true objective is\nunknown to most of the inner circle; they follow because the organization\npays well and the alternative is being identified as a former member.\nRook of Bramblewatch is one such agent — competent, conflicted, and looking\nfor an exit that does not involve dying.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "region-solasreach": {
    "text": "# The Kingdom of Solasreach\n\nThe human-majority Kingdom of Solasreach sprawls across the continent's central plains — golden farmland, rolling hills, and wide rivers under a sky that seems permanently bright. The capital Ríocht gleams white on the horizon, its cathedral spires visible for miles.\n\nSolasreach is the political heart of the continent: the Royal Court sets trends that ripple through every kingdom, the Adventurers' Guild maintains its headquarters here, and every merchant road leads eventually to its gates. Life is structured but not rigid — adventurers walk the same streets as knights, and a commoner with an S-rank card gets the same inn suite as a duchess.\n\nThe kingdom's underbelly runs just as deep: noble intrigues, Guild politics, and a hot-spring culture that makes private conversations very public. The Church of the Sacred Flame is influential but not absolute. The king holds audience beneath a ceiling painted with constellation maps and is widely considered competent, if cautious.\n\nRegional flavor leans bright and busy. Major settlements include Ríocht (capital), Dawnkeep (Royal Knights fortress), Silverfen (hot spring resort town), Goldfield (agricultural heartland), Grifonmere (lakefront noble retreat), Thornwall (eastern border fortress), Lion's Rest (retired knights' village), Hollow Cathedral (ruin), and the Pale Steppes (grasslands and horse clans).\n\n## Hidden Tension\nThe Royal Family's bloodline carries a dormant divine blessing that has\nnot manifested in three generations. The Church of the Sacred Flame\nsuspects this means the kingdom has lost its mandate — and is preparing\nto help the gods choose a new one. The horse clans of the Pale Steppes\ncarry the bloodline of the kingdom's founding queen, a fact the Royal\nFamily has spent centuries suppressing. If the clans ever united and\npressed their claim, the succession crisis would split Solasreach in half.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "region-demon-realm": {
    "text": "# The Demon Realm — Land and Atmosphere\n\nThe Demon Realm is not a wasteland. It is a place that has evolved in the presence of concentrated miasma — the ambient dark magical energy that saturates its geography the way mana saturates mortal lands, but denser, more volatile, more responsive to emotional state.\n\nThe landscape reflects this. Volcanic plains where the miasma vents are richest; obsidian mountain ranges that formed under centuries of concentrated power; forests of black and deep violet where the light filters through in ways that mortal eyes find beautiful in the way of things that are slightly wrong; rivers that run dark from mineral saturation and carry a low hum of magical charge; skies that cycle through deep purples and reds at dusk, never quite reaching the blue that mortal skies default to.\n\nNone of this is hostile by nature. The Demon Realm is habitable, productive, and in many regions prosperous. The demons who live here find the atmosphere energizing rather than oppressive. Mortals who enter experience it as intense — a pressure behind the eyes, an elevated heartbeat, emotions that feel slightly louder than usual. Extended exposure without miasma resistance adapts them gradually. Some mortals acclimate. Some find they prefer it.\n\nThe Demon Realm borders the mortal kingdoms at the frontier — a contested boundary that shifts with military and political pressure. The Demon Lord has held this boundary relatively stable for the past twelve years, which mortal kingdoms have decided means peace and which the Demon Lord's court knows means preparation.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "beastkin-appearance": {
    "text": "# Beastkin of Aerfála — Appearance Guide\n\nAll beastkin in Aerfála are kemonomimi — human in body with animal ears, a tail (or wings for Bird-Kin), and sometimes subtle markings like slit pupils, fangs, or scaled patches. They are NOT full anthropomorphic animals. A Cat-Kin has a human face, human hands, and a human body with cat ears and a cat tail. A Wolf-Kin has a human frame with pointed wolf ears and a thick tail. A Fox-Kin has large fox ears and a bushy tail. Bird-Kin have feathered wings sprouting from the back and sometimes feathered ear-tufts.\n\nWhen generating or describing beastkin characters and portraits: human body, animal ears, animal tail or wings. No fur covering the body. No animal snout. No paws instead of hands. The anime convention is ears-and-tail on an otherwise human form. This is consistent across all beastkin races in Aerfála.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "demon-seven-virtues": {
    "text": "# The Seven Virtues — Demon Cultural Values\n\nWhat mortal religions call the Seven Deadly Sins, the Demon Realm calls virtues — not in ironic inversion, but as a genuine cultural framework that developed independently and considers these qualities desirable expressions of a being's full nature.\n\nThis is not a formal caste system. Demons do not sort themselves into a single sin-category and live there. A demon embodies multiple virtues simultaneously and expresses different ones in different contexts. The framework is descriptive of cultural aspiration, not prescriptive of identity.\n\nPRIDE — The foundation. Knowing your worth and requiring others to acknowledge it. Not vanity — pride in demon culture is accurate self-assessment plus the refusal to pretend otherwise. A demon with no pride is considered broken or dangerous. Pride that is groundless and unchallenged is delusion, eventually corrected by reality or rivals.\n\nWRATH — The engine. Anger is honest. Anger is informative. A demon who never shows wrath either feels nothing or is hiding something; neither is trusted. Wrath expressed clearly and directed at its actual target is considered healthy. Wrath that festers, redirects, or explodes without cause is a problem of management, not of the emotion itself.\n\nLUST — The connector. Desire is acknowledged, not managed. Appetite for power, for beauty, for novelty, for achievement or new experience — all are expressions of lust in the demon understanding, and all are natural. The social regulation around lust is not suppression but conduct: who you pursue, how you pursue them, what you do with refusal.\n\nGLUTTONY — The appreciator. To consume with full attention and without apology — food, experience, knowledge, sensory intensity. A demon who holds back from the feast out of propriety is considered to be performing for someone else's benefit.\n\nGREED — The accumulator. Wanting more, and being honest about wanting more. Territory, power, objects of beauty or utility, relationships, information. A greedy demon who can actually acquire and hold what they want is admired. One who overreaches and loses is a cautionary example.\n\nENVY — The motivator. The gap between what you have and what you want is fuel, not shame. Unproductive envy that poisons without generating action is weakness. Envy that produces ambition is correct use of the emotion.\n\nSLOTH — The sovereign restraint. Not laziness — the deliberate decision to not engage, to conserve strength, to let a situation resolve itself. A high-ranking demon who is visibly unbothered by a threat they could end is demonstrating status through restraint-as-choice.\n\nCultural note: Restraint of any kind is not an automatic virtue. The mortal framework of \"the controlled person is the admirable one\" does not translate. Self-discipline in demon culture means knowing when to express fully and when to hold back as strategy — not performing moderation as a baseline good.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "faction-forge-lords": {
    "text": "# The Forge Lords of Dúnbheal\n\nThe Forge Lords are the aristocratic craft-guilds that govern Dúnbheal's economy and much of its politics. Each Forge Lord controls a sector — weapons, armor, tools, jewelry, enchanted goods — and holds their position through mastery of their craft and the loyalty of their apprentices.\n\nThe Forge Lords' Council debates policy in Ironhold's Chamber of Anvils, where tradition dictates that any decision must be unanimous or resolved by a forging contest. They are stubborn, proud, and produce the finest metalwork on the continent.\n\nForge Lord politics run on demonstrated mastery rather than birth. A young smith who out-forges a Lord in formal contest can claim the seat; a Lord who loses their edge can be displaced by their own apprentices. The system breeds excellence and rivalry in equal measure. Outsiders find the Forge Lords difficult to deal with — they respect craftsmanship and consider almost everything else a distraction.\n\n## Hidden Politics\nThe Forge Lords are sitting on a cache of pre-Collapse magitech — ancient\nblueprints for constructs and weapons that could reshape warfare. They are\ndivided on whether to use, sell, or destroy them. Brunhilde Ironhammer\nadvocates weaponization; the older Lords advocate concealment. The debate\nhas been ongoing for two decades and shows no sign of resolution. Brunhilde\nhas secretly reconstructed one of the blueprints and built a working\nprototype she has not tested.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "demi-plane-build-log": {
    "text": "# Demi-Plane Spatial Canon\n\nThe Demi-Plane contains a fixed 5×5 region grid:\n\n- y=-12: Still Frontier / Pale Reaches / North Field / Fold / Veiled Edge\n- y=-11: Veiled Fox Sanctuary / First Meadow / Threshold March / Stone Rise / Mirror Mere\n- y=-10: Quiet Shore / Westreach / Heartgate Basin / Dawn Barrow / Easthold\n- y=-9: Low Reaches / Hollow Vale / Deep Expanse / Southroll / Rift March\n- y=-8: Dim March / Grey Boundary / Open Horizon / Fade / Last Edge\n\nHeartgate Basin contains the Gateway Clearing, Gateway Chamber, Chamberlain's Quarters, Hearth Springs, Mana Heart, and Verdant Plot. The Veiled Fox Sanctuary contains the Hidden Estate of the Mother Fox.\n\nCoordinates, adjacency, travel direction, terrain, established areas, and map orientation remain stable. Access to the plane still requires Demi-Plane Gate or the Demi-Plane Anchor. Development reveals or enriches defined areas without rearranging the grid.",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0,
    "type": "worldLore"
  },
