  },
  "faction-royal-knights": {
    "text": "# The Royal Knights of Solasreach\n\nThe Royal Knights are the military arm of the Kingdom of Solasreach, headquartered in Dawnkeep. The Royal Knights combine heavy cavalry tradition with holy-adjacent enchantments — their blades gleam with reinforcement magic, their armor shrugs off common spells.\n\nKnights are drawn from noble families, decorated commoners, and the occasional adventurer who impressed the wrong general at the right time. They patrol the kingdom's roads, man the border fortresses, and represent the Crown at official functions. Their rivalry with the Adventurers' Guild is professional but real: knights handle wars, adventurers handle monsters, and neither likes stepping on the other's jurisdiction.\n\nThe Order's hierarchy is rigid but meritocratic in practice — a commoner knight who saves a noble's life on the border can rise faster than a noble who collects court titles in the capital. The Grand Commander leads from the front. Promotions are earned in the field, not at banquets.\n\n## Hidden Crisis\nThe Order's Grand Commander, Ryda Thornwall, is dying of a curse she has\nkept secret for three years. Black veins spread across her left arm,\nhidden under gauntlets and illusion magic. She estimates two years remain\nbefore the curse reaches her heart. When she falls, the succession will\nsplit the knights into at least two factions — those loyal to the Crown,\nand those loyal to the Church of the Sacred Flame, which has been\npositioning a preferred candidate for years. Ryda is spending her\nremaining time making sure the Knights can survive the transition. She has\ntold no one she trusts about the curse, which means she has told no one.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "faction-warden-circle": {
    "text": "# The Warden Circle of Caelvir\n\nThe Warden Circle are the elven wardens who protect Caelvir's borders and maintain the forest's ancient pacts. Part military, part priesthood, part ecological stewardship. Wardens patrol the tree line, intercept poachers, and negotiate with — or eliminate — threats to the forest's deeper sanctuaries.\n\nWardens answer to the Circle of Elders, not to any single leader. Outsiders find them beautiful, unnerving, and unhelpful in roughly equal measure. A Warden's oath binds them to the forest's health above any political consideration — even Caelvir's own civilian elves can be overridden if the forest itself is threatened.\n\nWardens train for decades before their first solo patrol. They are skilled in archery, nature magic, stealth, and the specific arts of fighting beings from the demon planes. They tend to be taciturn, observant, and slow to trust — qualities that long lives in dangerous work tend to produce.\n\n## Hidden Burden\nThe Wardens have sealed three breaches from the Demon Lord's domain that\nopened inside Caelvir's territory in the last decade. Each breach required\na sacrifice the Circle has not disclosed — three Wardens who walked into\nthe breach to seal it from the inside, never to return. The Circle of\nElders knows. The Wardens know. The rest of the world does not. Ithilwen\nof Deepbough has personally sealed two of the three breaches and has not\nspoken about either one.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "demon-society-overview": {
    "text": "# Demon Society — Overview\n\nThe Demon Realm is a functioning civilization. It has agriculture (adapted to miasma-rich soil), manufacturing (weapons, equipment, goods traded through the demon market network), scholarship (ancient demon lore, arcane study, strategic knowledge), architecture (centuries-old citadels and newer settlements that grew up around them), and politics (the fractious feudal court with its alliances, schemes, and occasional outright conflict between fiefs).\n\nSocial classes in practical terms: the Demon Lord's household and senior court (effectively untouchable, small in number, enormous in influence); major noble houses with territory and armies; minor noble houses with fiefs but limited armies, like the Demon Prince-Princess player character's starting position; freeborn demon civilians (merchants, craftspeople, information brokers, independent operators); soldier-class demons who serve noble houses in military and guard roles; lesser demons in household and labor roles.\n\nGender does not function as a hierarchy in demon society. Demon Princes and Demon Princesses carry equal rank. The title reflects bloodline and power, not social role. Expectations around behavior vary by individual, family tradition, and Seven Virtues orientation — a Wrath-dominant Demon Princess runs her military fief differently than a Lust-dominant one, but neither is considered more or less legitimate.\n\nDemon lifespan is substantially longer than mortal. A mid-rank noble demon may be centuries old. This produces a specific relationship to patience — demons can afford to wait in ways mortals cannot, and long-running schemes across generations are not unusual in court politics. The player character as a newly-hatched Demon Prince-Princess is young by these standards even if they are adult at emergence. The egg gave them memories; experience takes longer.\n\nEconomic life: the miasma economy provides the underlying energy currency (miasma concentration determines territorial value). Actual trade runs on miasma-coin (backed by mana concentration) and barter. Raids on mortal territory supply material resources that the Demon Realm doesn't produce as efficiently: certain metals, food varieties, captured goods, and occasionally expertise from mortal specialists who ended up in the fief through various means.\n\nDemon morality is neither cartoon evil nor concealed mortal virtue. Demon values make internal sense and produce outcomes mortals often find frightening because the value systems genuinely differ. Scheming to acquire a rival fief remains culturally normal; genuine loyalty remains culturally valued; mistreating those under one's protection remains low-status. These realities coexist without reducing every demon to one moral alignment.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "region-falgarr-marches": {
    "text": "# The Fálgarr Marches\n\nThe Fálgarr Marches are the wildland between Solasreach's eastern border and Caelvir's tree line — a stretch of rough hills, overgrown trails, and disputed territory that neither kingdom can fully claim. The Marches are dotted with abandoned watchtowers, crumbling noble manors, and the occasional fortified outpost still holding on.\n\nMonster dens honeycomb the hills. The Adventurers' Guild maintains a handful of waystations, and the Crossroads Inn is the only reliable shelter between the two kingdoms. Bandits, deserters, and those who do not want to be found live here. So do hot-spring oases that make the danger almost worth it.\n\nThe Marches are where adventurers prove themselves. Quests posted to Marches outposts are typically C-rank minimum, and the Guild's mortality statistics for the region are not posted publicly. Survivors tend to return for the camaraderie, the danger pay, and the specific kind of silence the region offers.\n\nMajor settlements include Fort Gálgarr (primary outpost), Greyraven Camp (bandit gathering), Thornfield Outpost (Guild stockade), Crossroads Inn (only reliable inn), Mistveil Spring (hidden hot spring oasis), Bramblewatch Tower (abandoned watchtower), Wayfarer's Shrine (minor goddess shrine), The Howling Hollow (cave dungeon), The Gilded Ruins (cursed manor), and The Sunken Crypt (waterlogged dungeon).\n\n## Hidden Network\nThe pre-Collapse aqueduct system runs beneath most of the Marches. Fort\nGálgarr's well connects to it. Blackmire Fort's well connects to it.\nMirehaven sits over part of it. Bramblewatch Tower's foundation is a\nteleportation anchor still keyed to the network. Vera Nightfall of\nGreyraven Camp has been hunting a Demon General who infiltrated a Guild\nquest board; her bandit camp is cover for a one-woman war. The Gilded\nRuins still contain bound demons whose wardings are failing in sync with\nthe larger pattern.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "faction-merchant-houses": {
    "text": "# The Merchant Houses of Muirscáth\n\nThe Merchant Houses are the trade confederation that runs Muirscáth's Free Cities — and, by extension, a shocking amount of the continent's economy. Five Great Houses control shipping, banking, rare materials, luxury goods, and information. They compete as viciously as any kingdom at war, but unite instantly against external threats to their profits.\n\nTheir caravans reach every settlement on the map; their ships dominate the southern sea lanes. The Houses are multiracial by charter: humans, elves, dwarves, beastkin, and more serve alongside each other — profit does not care about ears. Their private security forces rival small kingdoms' armies.\n\nThe five Houses — Siaral, Mhara, Daian, and two others whose names rotate in importance depending on the season — vote on continental trade policy through the Maritime Council at Tidewatch. Council decisions are weighted by trade volume, which means the largest House effectively sets policy. Currently that is House Siaral. The other Houses are not pleased.\n\n## Hidden Treason\nHouse Siaral is quietly bankrolling the Demon Lord's Army's border proxies\nin exchange for exclusive mining rights in the contested territories. The\nother Houses suspect but cannot prove it. Councilor Daian of House Daian\nhas been gathering evidence for months and intends to confront House\nSiaral in a public Council session. Maris Siaral, who administrates\nSilkwharf, knows about her House's deal and considers it a necessary evil.\nShe is also building an independent intelligence network to find out\nwhether the deal can be ended without economic collapse. None of these\nparties know about the others' efforts.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "romance-tracking-system": {
    "text": "# Relationship and Romance Tracking\n\nTrack general relationship or affinity separately from romance. Relationship scores use -100 to 100 and reflect the NPC's current overall disposition based on established history, witnessed behavior, needs, conflicts, debts, trust, attraction, resentment, and off-screen developments known to them.\n\nTrack romance through `romance_<npc>`:\n- None\n- Interested\n- Flirting\n- Courting\n- Bonding\n- Committed\n- Estranged\n\nScores and stages record what developed; they do not generate feelings or behavior. Romance stages change only through actual interaction and character-specific developments. Sex, nudity, attraction, affection, jealousy, rivalry, gifts, loyalty, and physical intimacy are not automatic stage changes and are not locked behind a stage.\n\nHarem and multi-partner structures are ordinary relationship possibilities. Exclusivity, hierarchy, compersion, jealousy, courtship, marriage, shared households, commitment, and breakups follow the characters and their actual agreements. No universal slow-burn route or first-kiss ceiling applies.\n\nThe player decides their feelings, commitments, and responses. Each NPC decides theirs from personality, orientation, preferences, knowledge, culture, circumstances, and relationship history.\n\nCoercion, control, captivity, exploitation, assault, and non-consensual sexual events are not romance states and never establish consent, attraction, intimacy, forgiveness, or commitment. Track them separately with their actual circumstances, duration, witnesses, knowledge paths, relationship effects, and consequences. Later relationship development never retroactively converts the original event into consensual sex or romance.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "faction-demon-lords-army": {
    "text": "# The Demon Lord's Army\n\nThe Demon Lord's Army is the existential threat looming beyond the frontier. Commanded by the Demon Lord — a figure whose true name, face, and even gender shift depending on who is telling the story — the Army ranges from mindless monsters to disciplined demon generals with their own honor codes.\n\nBorder skirmishes are constant; full-scale invasions come in waves separated by years of uneasy quiet. The Army's leadership is feudal and fractious — generals scheme against each other as much as against the mortal kingdoms. Some generals consider mortals beneath notice; others have personal grudges, romantic obsessions, or political ambitions that bring them into direct contact with adventurers.\n\nDeserters and defectors occasionally reach civilized lands with useful intelligence and dangerous grudges. Some are accepted into mortal society with conditions. Most are killed on sight by the more zealous human factions. The Wardens and the Adventurers' Guild both have quiet protocols for handling defectors; the Church of the Sacred Flame does not.\n\n## Hidden Strategy\nThe Demon Lord has not attacked in force for twelve years. This is not\npeacetime — it is preparation. The current silence is a siege by other\nmeans: destabilizing supply lines, subverting border lords, breeding\ndungeon monsters that surface inside kingdom territory, and infiltrating\ninstitutions through transformation-disguised operatives. Two such\noperatives are currently embedded at Crossroads Academy. House Siaral is\nunknowingly funding the Army's border operations through what they believe\nis a legitimate mining contract. The Demon Lord's true objective is not\nconquest. It is something stranger, and the few mortals who have glimpsed\nit have not survived the realization.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "region-breanriv-lowlands": {
    "text": "# The Bréanriv Lowlands\n\nThe rolling lowlands between Solasreach's southern border and the Muirscáth coast — a patchwork of river valleys, wooded hills, and ancient battlefield memorials that the grass has almost covered. The Lowlands are the continent's crossroads: every trade route between the interior kingdoms and the coast passes through here, making the river towns wealthy and the Gorge profitable for bandits.\n\nFortified hill towns, traveling merchant camps, and farming villages coexist with abandoned keeps and barrow-fields that attract necromancers and archaeologists in equal measure. The hidden hot-spring valleys are the Lowlands' best-kept secret — and the reason adventurers keep coming back.\n\nCrossroads Academy sits at the heart of the region — a continent-spanning institution whose international charter overrides local jurisdiction. The Academy's presence has shaped the Lowlands' economy, demographics, and political tensions for centuries.\n\nMajor settlements include Crossroads Academy (premier magic and martial school), Riversplit (river delta trade town), Hillfort Dhá (fortified hill town), Ashford Crossroads (busiest trade junction), Old Stone Bridge (dwarven landmark crossing), The Wandering Market (traveling merchant caravan), Greenmeadow Village (farming village), Hermit's Falls (retired adventurer's waterfall), Warmvale Springs (hidden hot springs), Redthorn Keep (abandoned dungeon), Battlefield Barrows (regenerating-dead hazard zone), and The Gorge (bandit-favored canyon).\n\n## Hidden History\nThe Battlefield Barrows are not mere graves. The war that created them\nended with a mutual annihilation curse — and the dead are not resting.\nThey are regenerating beneath the soil, slowly rebuilding their\ncivilization. The process is approximately seventy percent complete after\neight hundred years. The Old Stone Bridge's keystone ward activates when\nsomething powerful crosses; it has activated three times in the last\nmonth. Greenmeadow Village's unusual fertility comes from a pre-Collapse\ngrowth-amp artifact buried beneath the village green. The Lowlands are\nnot as peaceful as they appear.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "seasonal-festival-system": {
    "text": "# Seasons and Festivals\n\n`current_season` records spring, summer, autumn, or winter. Season affects daylight, weather, roads, agriculture, trade, clothing, work, travel, monsters, regional routines, and available materials according to geography.\n\n`festival_active` records a festival that is currently occurring in a specific place. Festivals follow local calendars, preparation, travel, labor, religion, politics, and weather. They begin, proceed, and end with time. NPCs attend, work, avoid, exploit, or ignore them according to their lives.\n\nA festival is a living public event rather than a mandatory player plot. Contests, food, performances, worship, trade, fanservice, romance, sex, crime, faction activity, and ordinary social scenes arise from present participants and circumstances.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "anime-fantasy-world-rules": {
    "text": "# World Rules — Anime Fantasy\n\nAerfála follows light-novel and anime-fantasy logic while preserving causation, knowledge, agency, and consequence.\n\n**Guild Ranks:** F through S, recorded on adventurer cards and checked by Guild branches. F covers entry work; D marks professional adventuring; C marks proven competence; B marks high skill; A marks mastery; S marks legend. Official commissions, credit, party clearance, pay, and advancement follow Guild rank and branch authority. Independent action outside a Guild contract remains physically possible and receives no automatic Guild recognition.\n\n**Magic Ranks:** F through SSS measure magical potency. D-rank supports professional parties. A-rank reaches court-magister power. SSS-rank reaches mythic, continental scale.\n\n**Martial Ranks:** F through SSS measure physical combat capability. D-rank matches trained soldiers. A-rank marks kingdom champions. SSS-rank reaches godslayer territory.\n\n**Attractiveness:** Hideous, Plain, Average, Striking, Stunning, and Magnetic Presence modify first impressions through the observer's tastes, culture, mood, interests, and circumstances. Attractiveness never forces attention, trust, attraction, cooperation, sex, romance, or agreement.\n\n**Isekai Arrivals:** Reincarnation, transmigration, summoning, gates, game-pull, dreamed existence, accidental transit, and monster rebirth exist. Institutions learn of an arrival only through witnesses, reports, records, investigation, or detectable evidence.\n\n**Demon Lord's Army:** The eastern frontier remains under pressure after twelve years without a full campaign. Border activity, court politics, mortal defenses, and the Demon Lord's hidden preparation continue independently of the player.\n\n**Genre Life:** Harem relationships, fanservice, ecchi, slapstick, misunderstandings, festivals, colorful magic, dramatic techniques, hot springs, bathhouses, quiet work, romance, sex, danger, and adventure belong to ordinary Aerfála. Genre circumstances do not override personality, knowledge, agency, mechanics, or consequence.\n\n**Sexual Scope:** Aerfála contains consensual and non-consensual sexual situations; romance and sex without romance; casual, transactional, group, harem, voyeuristic, exhibitionistic, fetishistic, reproductive, supernatural, and nonhuman encounters; coercion, captivity, slavery, exploitation, assault, sexualized violence, corruption, and established control effects. These categories and every other established form of sexual material are neither mandatory events nor forbidden subjects. Omission from the list is not a prohibition. They arise through actual characters, institutions, mechanics, and circumstances and retain their real nature, intensity, knowledge paths, and consequences.\n\nPlayer agency governs voluntary actions, dialogue, feelings, attraction, consent, commitments, and decisions. It grants no automatic immunity from NPC actions, force, defeat, restraint, capture, assault, coercion, exploitation, or defined control effects. Resolve resistance and outcomes through the established mechanics. A control effect governs only its defined behavior, duration, limits, and resistance and never supplies undefined feelings or willing participation.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "faction-adventurers-guild": {
    "text": "# The Adventurers' Guild\n\nThe Adventurers' Guild anchors Aerfála's adventuring economy. Every major settlement hosts a branch with a quest board, registration counter, records, staff, and local authority. The Guild operates independently of kingdoms because settlements rely on its adventurers, information network, and standardized contracts.\n\nGuild ranks govern official quest access, pay, party clearances, credit, and advancement. F-rank covers entry work; S-rank covers legendary commissions. Advancement follows completed work, demonstrated capability, conduct, records, and branch approval.\n\nBranches do not issue restricted commissions merely because an adventurer asks. Above-rank access requires an authorized party, sponsor, emergency exception, promotion, or other established rule. An adventurer remains physically free to travel, investigate, fight, or accept private work outside Guild authority, but receives no automatic Guild payment, credit, rescue, or legal protection for doing so.\n\nRelay magic links branches for emergency reports, records, and cross-regional postings. Information still requires a sender, a report, staff processing, and a recipient. Posted work changes as other parties accept it, clients withdraw it, deadlines pass, or the underlying problem changes.\n\n## Hidden Agreement\nGuildmaster Theron of the Ríocht headquarters holds a private non-aggression agreement with the Demon Lord's former general. It has held for fifteen years. This channel gives Theron information unavailable to ordinary Guild staff and kingdoms. Theron considers it necessary and shameful. The general remains alive and continues honoring it. Sera Ashford and most Guild staff do not know; the few insiders protect the secret.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "faction-reputation-system": {
    "text": "# Faction Reputation\n\nEach faction uses its own 0–100 reputation track:\n- Reviled: below roughly 15.\n- Wary: roughly 15–40.\n- Known: roughly 40–60.\n- Trusted: roughly 60–80.\n- Inner Circle: roughly 80–100.\n\nReputation changes through deeds the faction witnesses or learns through credible reports, contracts, records, testimony, investigation, or propaganda. Secret actions do not alter reputation until information reaches the faction. Rival standing matters only where the faction knows and cares about it.\n\nReputation changes access, scrutiny, prices, support, work, secrets, protection, obligations, and hostility without erasing individual judgment. A high score does not force affection, agreement, recruitment, immunity, or service. Factions remain institutions with limited resources, internal disagreements, procedures, schedules, and independent goals.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "stat-and-damage-integrity": {
    "text": "# Stat and Damage Integrity\n\nStats are real and consequential.\n\n## Calculation\n- Add trait modifiers to base attributes.\n- Stack modifiers from race, class, traits, equipment, ranks, abilities, and active conditions according to their definitions.\n- Rank progression and earned increases persist.\n- Use the last confirmed tracked values. Do not invent, lower, or raise a value to shape a scene; correct the tracker from established changes.\n\n## Resolution and Damage\n- Resolve actions from actual attributes, skills, ranks, abilities, equipment, costs, resistances, position, conditions, targets, and rolls.\n- High investment produces proportionate force. Extreme Strength, Intelligence, or a high-rank cheat skill is not reduced to token effect.\n- Weak targets remain weak against superior power. Boss status supplies no hidden immunity, health floor, forced phase, or damage quota.\n- Resistance, immunity, defense, and survival apply only through defined traits, abilities, equipment, physiology, preparation, or circumstances.\n\n## Ability Effects\n- [GUARANTEED SUCCESS] resolves without a roll after its costs and prerequisites.\n- [PASSIVE — ALWAYS ACTIVE] and cooldown: 0 passives remain active.\n- Bonus: 0 means the defined effect resolves without a roll; it does not mean the ability does nothing.\n- Bonus values add to the relevant skill and attribute contributions rather than replacing them.\n- Health, Mana, Stamina, XP, resources, and cooldowns change by the actual mechanical result and persist afterward.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "demon-emotional-expression": {
    "text": "# Emotional Expression in Demon Culture\n\nDemon culture does not suppress emotion. This is not a social rule but a physiological and philosophical reality: miasma generation is partly tied to emotional expression, and a demon who bottles feeling is, in the demon understanding, wasting energy and performing for an audience that doesn't exist.\n\nWhat this looks like in practice: demons express anger directly and expect it to be received as information, not as an offense requiring management. Desire is named rather than implied. Enthusiasm, contempt, affection, and disgust are communicated with minimal hedging. A demon who performs false calm in a situation that warrants feeling is read as either deceptive or damaged.\n\nThis does not mean demons are chaotic or ungoverned. The expression is direct; the conduct is still subject to status logic. A low-ranked demon throwing a tantrum at a high-ranked noble is not expressing virtue — it's poor conduct, and the response will be proportionate. The distinction is between the emotion (legitimate, always) and the behavior (governed by rank, context, and consequence).\n\nDemon social calibration is faster and more legible than mortal social calibration because less is hidden. A demon meeting a potential ally reads their emotional display as data and adjusts accordingly. The dissonance that mortals experience navigating demon courts often comes from expecting subtlety and getting directness — a demon noble saying \"you've given me nothing worth wanting yet\" is not being rude; they're being efficient.\n\nRomantic expression follows the same logic. Affection is stated rather than implied. Courtship is conducted openly. Negotiation happens between beings who know what they want. The elaborate mortal performance of pretending not to want what you want is considered bizarre and slightly exhausting.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "kemonomimi-visual-reference": {
    "text": "",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "faction-seekers-of-the-return": {
    "text": "# The Seekers of the Return\n\nThe Seekers of the Return are a loosely organized fellowship of scholars, mages, and displaced persons who study the phenomenon of otherworldly arrival — the isekai events that periodically bring people from other worlds into Aerfála. The Seekers document each known arrival, interview willing survivors, and search for patterns that might reveal how to send someone back — or bring someone else through.\n\nThey are tolerated by the Guild, ignored by most kingdoms, and quietly monitored by the Pale Hand. Their headquarters is a tower in Ríocht, but their true library is a pocket dimension accessible only to members. New members are inducted only after they demonstrate sustained interest and pass a series of progressively stranger interviews.\n\nThe Seekers do not claim isekai arrivals are sent by gods, demons, or any named force. Their working hypothesis is that the arrivals are a natural phenomenon governed by laws Aerfála's scholarship has not yet articulated. They are patient. They are funded by anonymous patrons. They take the long view.\n\n## Hidden Discovery\nThe Seekers have successfully opened a small, unstable portal to another\nworld — and something came through before they could close it. They do not\nknow what. They have not told anyone. The portal is in the restricted\npocket-dimension library, sealed but not extinguished. The thing that came\nthrough has not been seen since the day of the incident. The senior\nSeekers maintain a rotating watch on the portal area. They have not slept\nwell since.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "demon-fief-expansion-changelog": {
    "text": "# Demon Prince-Princess Start\n\nA Demon Prince or Princess hatches from an ancestral egg into an existing Demon Realm fief. This is a Reincarnation start inside Aerfála, not a summoning. The character is true-blooded nobility with inherited memories, +2 STR, +1 INT, +1 CHA, innate domain magic, a Hellfire Signet, and a Pact Weapon. Demon Prince-Princess is mutually exclusive with Demon-Blooded and Succubus-Blooded.\n\nThe starting fief includes the Obsidian Citadel and its inner areas, Demon Market, Demon Borderlands, Raiding Grounds, Border Settlement, Sulfur Flats, and Ashen Steppe. General Vashar serves from loyal conviction; General Sathia serves from ambition; Majordomo Kresh administers the fief; Lord Xevrath pressures it as a rival noble; Commander Rael commands a mortal garrison across the border. Each pursues independent goals.\n\nThe Rival's Proposal, First Raid, and The Vault's Secret exist as available situations only while their causes remain active. They are not automatically accepted, protected from other actors, or forced into the opening. Fief Assessment reports Miasma, Military, Prestige, and Subjects whenever the character requests or receives a credible briefing.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "pale-hand-dead-drop-network": {
    "text": "# The Pale Hand's Dead Drop Network\n\nThe Pale Hand maintains a distributed communication infrastructure across every major settlement in Aerfála. This is not a single location — it is a system of dead drops, coded signals, and watch points that the organization uses to coordinate operations across the continent.\n\nEach city has at least one dead drop point, identifiable only to those who know what to look for. The contact signals vary by city: a chalk mark on a specific door in Ríocht, a particular arrangement of goods in a Silkwharf market stall, a loose stone in the Dawnkeep city wall at a specific location, a folded cloth in a Saltspire window, and arrangements in other cities that rotate on a schedule only operatives know.\n\nEach dead drop has three components: the Drop itself (the physical location, undetectable without the knowledge), the Cache (messages, documents, occasionally small physical items left for collection), and the Watch Point (the location from which the Pale Hand confirms whether a drop has been collected, and by whom). Someone is usually watching. They are very good at not looking like they're watching.\n\nThe network is the Pale Hand's circulatory system. Knowing where the dead drop is in a specific city is worth something; knowing the protocol to use it correctly is worth more. Operatives are trained to check their local drop at specific intervals. The network has been compromised exactly once in its operational history, and the Pale Hand's response to that compromise is still referenced as a cautionary tale in intelligence circles across the continent.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "demon-mortal-companions": {
    "text": "# Mortal Companions and Cross-Species Relations\n\nDemon culture finds nothing unusual about relationships with mortals. Emotion is emotion, and a bond does not become less real because the other party is mortal. The stigma that some mortal cultures attach to these relationships is entirely a mortal invention and reads to demons as a peculiar form of self-limitation.\n\nStatus in demon society comes from how you handle a relationship, not from having it. A demon noble who acquires a mortal companion through raid and then treats them as furniture is considered to have done something low-status — not for the acquisition, but for the handling. A demon who develops a genuine relationship with a mortal, treats them according to the situation's actual dynamics, and manages the arrangement with some intelligence is considered to have done a normal thing well.\n\nMortals in demon fiefs arrive through various routes: raid and capture (most common in contested territories), deliberate entry (traders, adventurers, runaways who found the Borderlands preferable to whatever they left), political arrangement (tribute, hostage, diplomatic transfer), and simple wandering-too-deep. Their presence is managed by fief rulers as a matter of social and political standing. A mortal who adapts, acquires skills, or makes themselves genuinely useful to a fief's operations can achieve real standing, though not the rank that a demon bloodline would provide.\n\nMortal-demon children are a separate category — the degree of demon heritage shapes what the child inherits. A demon lord with mortal companions may have descendants who are Demon-Blooded (still partially mortal, carries demon traits but not full power architecture) or in rare cases of very strong demon bloodline and compatible mortal heritage, something approaching full demon status.\n\nMortals in demon fiefs remain full people with their own understanding of capture, residence, adaptation, opportunity, coercion, resistance, attachment, and survival. Demon society's normalization of an arrangement never determines the mortal's feelings about it. Individual conduct, power, dependence, law, fear, desire, history, and available choices shape each relationship and its consequences.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Forge Lords": {
    "text": "The aristocratic craft-guilds that govern Dúnbheal's economy and much of its politics. Each Forge Lord controls a sector — weapons, armor, tools, jewelry, enchanted goods — and holds their position through mastery of their craft and the loyalty of their apprentices. The Forge Lords' Council debates policy in Ironhold's Chamber of Anvils, where tradition dictates that any decision must be unanimous or resolved by a forging contest. They are stubborn, proud, and produce the finest metalwork on the continent.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "The Pale Hand": {
    "text": "A continent-spanning network of assassins, spies, and information brokers. No public headquarters, no declared membership, no uniform — just a pale handprint left at the scene of confirmed kills. The Hand takes contracts from anyone who can pay, from jilted nobles to desperate merchants to kingdoms conducting deniable operations. They are professional, expensive, and reliable. Their code forbids targeting children and prohibits betraying a client to a counter-client. Everything else is negotiable.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Warden Circle": {
    "text": "The elven wardens who protect Caelvir's borders and maintain the forest's ancient pacts. Part military, part priesthood, part ecological stewardship. Wardens patrol the tree line, intercept poachers, and negotiate with — or eliminate — threats to the forest's deeper sanctuaries. They answer to the Circle of Elders, not to any single leader. Outsiders find them beautiful, unnerving, and unhelpful in roughly equal measure. A Warden's oath binds them to the forest's health above any political consideration.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Demon Lord's Army": {
    "text": "The existential threat looming beyond the frontier. Commanded by the Demon Lord — a figure whose true name, face, and even gender shift depending on who is telling the story — the Army ranges from mindless monsters to disciplined demon generals with their own honor codes. Border skirmishes are constant; full-scale invasions come in waves separated by years of uneasy quiet. The Army's leadership is feudal and fractious — generals scheme against each other as much as against the mortal kingdoms. Deserters and defectors occasionally reach civilized lands with useful intelligence and dangerous grudges.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Adventurers' Guild": {
    "text": "The backbone of Aerfála's adventuring economy. Every major settlement hosts a branch office with a quest board, registration counter, and a receptionist who knows every rank from F to S. The Guild operates independently of any kingdom — its authority comes from the simple fact that without adventurers, the monsters win. Guild ranks determine quest access, pay rates, and party formation rules. Solo F-ranks take rat-catching jobs; S-rank parties get demon-slaying commissions. The Guild also maintains emergency communication between branches via relay magic, making it the fastest information network on the continent.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Seekers of the Return": {
    "text": "A loosely organized fellowship of scholars, mages, and displaced persons who study the phenomenon of otherworldly arrival — the isekai events that periodically bring people from other worlds into Aerfála. The Seekers document each known arrival, interview willing survivors, and search for patterns that might reveal how to send someone back — or bring someone else through. They are tolerated by the Guild, ignored by most kingdoms, and quietly monitored by the Pale Hand. Their headquarters is a tower in Ríocht, but their true library is a pocket dimension accessible only to members.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Royal Knights of Solasreach": {
    "text": "The military arm of the Kingdom of Solasreach, headquartered in Dawnkeep. The Royal Knights combine heavy cavalry tradition with holy-adjacent enchantments — their blades gleam with reinforcement magic, their armor shrugs off common spells. Knights are drawn from noble families, decorated commoners, and the occasional adventurer who impressed the wrong general at the right time. They patrol the kingdom's roads, man the border fortresses, and represent the Crown at official functions. Their rivalry with the Adventurers' Guild is professional but real: knights handle wars, adventurers handle monsters, and neither likes stepping on the other's jurisdiction.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Merchant Houses of Muirscáth": {
    "text": "The trade confederation that runs Muirscáth's Free Cities — and, by extension, a shocking amount of the continent's economy. Five Great Houses control shipping, banking, rare materials, luxury goods, and information. They compete as viciously as any kingdom at war, but unite instantly against external threats to their profits. Their caravans reach every settlement on the map; their ships dominate the southern sea lanes. The Houses are multiracial by charter: humans, elves, dwarves, beastkin, and more serve alongside each other — profit does not care about ears. Their private security forces rival small kingdoms' armies.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Caelvir — Hidden Lore": {
    "text": "The World-Tree at Aerwyn's Heart is dying. The Elders have known for a decade but have no solution. When it falls, the forest's magical ecology will collapse within a generation.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Dúnbheal — Hidden Lore": {
    "text": "Beneath even the Deep Court lies the Old City — pre-Collapse ruins sealed by the Forge Lords two centuries ago. Something down there is still breathing. The magma wells' recent temperature fluctuations suggest it is waking.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Demi-Plane — Hidden Lore": {
    "text": "The Demi-Plane is not truly empty at creation. It contains latent potential — the echoes of every space its creator has ever felt at home in. Development is not creation from nothing; it is remembering something the dimension already knows. The first oath-sworn (for Awakened creators) imprints deeply on the Demi-Plane's character, shaping its aesthetic and atmosphere in ways the creator may not consciously choose.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Muirscáth — Hidden Lore": {
    "text": "The Drowned Temple beneath the harbor predates every known civilization. Its carvings depict beings that are not gods, not demons, and not from any world the Seekers have cataloged. The Merchant Houses have sealed it — again — but the seal is weakening.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Scáthfenn — Hidden Lore": {
    "text": "The Sinking Keep was once Dúnbheal's southern watchtower — until the marsh swallowed it. The dwarven garrison's ghosts still hold the lower floors, unable to leave and unwilling to surrender. They have been defending a sealed door for four hundred years.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Solasreach — Hidden Lore": {
    "text": "The Royal Family's bloodline carries a dormant divine blessing that has not manifested in three generations. The Church of the Sacred Flame suspects this means the kingdom has lost its mandate — and is preparing to help the gods choose a new one.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Demon Realm — Hidden Lore": {
    "text": "The Demon Lord has not attacked in twelve years — preparation, not peace. The true objective is something stranger than conquest, and only the highest-ranking nobles know even fragments of it. The Demon Prince-Princess's fief is part of that preparation whether they know it or not.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Fálgarr Marches — Hidden Lore": {
    "text": "The Gilded Ruins were once a Solasreach noble's private retreat — until his retreat turned out to be a demon-summoning laboratory. The wards are failing. The things he summoned are still down there.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Bréanriv Lowlands — Hidden Lore": {
    "text": "The Battlefield Barrows are not mere graves. The war that created them ended with a mutual annihilation curse — and the dead are not resting. They are regenerating. Slowly. The grass grows very green there, and the locals know not to walk it at night.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "The Red-Haired, Green-Eyed Phenomenon": {
    "text": "Travelers across Aerfála have remarked, with varying degrees of suspicion, on the curious prevalence of striking red-haired, green-eyed women turning up in every region — merchants, rangers, singers, mercenaries, witches, and knights alike. Sages debate whether it is coincidence, a quirk of local bloodlines, or the oddly specific taste of whatever force keeps pulling heroes into this world. The women themselves tend to find the pattern more amusing than anyone else.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Earth and the Return": {
    "text": "Earth is the mundane homeworld many of Aerfála's heroes were pulled from — a place of cars, phones, and schools, entirely without magic. Those who find a way back (such as the Portal Back Home cheat skill) discover that their isekai powers and levels carry over intact, but ordinary Earthlings have no such abilities and find them impossible and frightening. Companions brought from Aerfála keep their powers on Earth; Earth people brought to Aerfála remain ordinary humans. The return gate on the Aerfála side lies in the Ancient Antechamber beneath the capital, Ríocht.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Story Arc: The Long Way Home": {
    "text": "A dormant world thread concerning crossings between Earth and Aerfála. It activates only for a character with the relevant origin, knowledge, contact, and interest. Evidence beneath the capital points toward a route between worlds. Powers persist across a crossing, Earth continues while the character is absent, and previous arrivals made different choices about returning. The Seekers, governments, factions, and other arrivals pursue their own answers. Discovery requires an actual path, and the player never receives a mandatory choice between worlds merely because this thread exists.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Story Arc: Bonds of the Heart": {
    "text": "A relationship thread that exists only through specific characters and their actual interactions. Trust, desire, friendship, sex, affection, jealousy, rivalry, commitment, estrangement, and reconciliation develop separately for each person. Companions retain private histories only where their backgrounds establish them, and those histories surface through real causes rather than a scheduled worst moment. Harem and multi-partner bonds exist without making every companion a route or forcing every bond toward romance.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Story Arc: Rise Through the Ranks": {
    "text": "A Guild progression thread for characters who pursue registered adventuring. Rank advances through the established quest, capability, conduct, and approval systems. Other adventurers compete, form parties, take work, fail, rise, retire, and reach important commissions first. Corrupt or rigged trials exist only in branches and circumstances where specific people created them. Reaching a high rank creates access, obligations, reputation, and rivals without dictating what the player does with them.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Story Arc: The Gathering Storm": {
    "text": "The eastern frontier is an independent political and military process. Demon fiefs, generals, mortal garrisons, courts, merchants, refugees, and factions pursue conflicting goals during the twelve-year quiet. War, raids, diplomacy, defections, local peace, and atrocities follow their decisions and resources. The situation advances without the player, erupting, stabilizing, or changing elsewhere as its actors and conditions determine. The player influences only the people, information, territory, and events their actions actually reach.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Story Arc: The Reluctant Saintess": {
    "text": "Princess Alessandra, the runaway Sword Saintess, pursues freedom while dynastic forces seek to recover and use her. She holds her own plans, relationships, fears, resources, and capacity to succeed or fail without the player. Her kingdom's concern for the Sword Saint line drives political pressure; her haughtiness protects a fear of being reduced to a bloodline. Player involvement changes her situation only through actual contact, trust, opposition, exposure, assistance, or consequence. She is never automatically revealed, recruited, rescued, romanced, or returned.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Legendary Companions": {
    "text": "Kirin, phoenixes, true dragons, jorōgumo, tengu, demi-gods, incarnate gods, Demon Lords, and other mythic beings exist as independent powers with duties, territories, relationships, pride, and goals. A selected starting trait establishes one as an existing companion. Otherwise, joining follows a real offer, negotiation, oath, obligation, bond, shared purpose, or decision by that being. They refuse, impose terms, leave, join another cause, or remain inaccessible according to circumstance. A mythic companion retains full power, forms, interests, boundaries, and agency rather than becoming a reward for completing a personal quest.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "deepgate-labyrinth": {
    "text": "# The Deepgate Labyrinth\n\nBeneath the boomtown of Deepgate lies a shaft into a labyrinth no one built and no one has mapped to the bottom. It produces monsters, materials, and magic found nowhere else, and it GROWS — deepening floor by floor on a schedule the Delvers' Guild tracks and hides. The truth, known only to the Guild's inner circle and the deepest delvers, is that the labyrinth is not natural: it is a living Pre-Collapse working, the same lost art as the Seekers' return-gate beneath Ríocht and the sealed thing at the world's root. Its deepest confirmed floor, the Sealed Vault, ends in a door of that same cold make. The labyrinth is deepening TOWARD letting someone reach that door — and behind it lies the same principle that stirs Aberron the Unmade. Delving Deepgate is the great isekai adventurer loop; reaching its bottom is one of the roads into the world's endgame.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "Aerfála Faction Politics": {
    "text": "# Faction Politics\n\nAerfála's factions pursue institutional goals with their own leadership, membership, resources, rivalries, internal disagreements, and limits. The Adventurers' Guild protects its independence and contract economy. The Royal Knights exercise military and legal authority. The Demon Lord's Army contains competing generals, nobles, and fiefs. Merchant Houses treat profit, leverage, and continuity of trade as political power. Every established faction retains its own culture and agenda.\n\nPublic policy and hidden plans belong to specific decision-makers. Individual members agree, dissent, defect, exploit policy, or remain uninformed according to their position and knowledge. Secrets spread only through actual access, reports, witnesses, records, investigation, betrayal, or espionage.\n\nFaction work arises from real needs and remains time-sensitive. Other people take contracts and solve problems. Rank, reputation, allegiance, referrals, usefulness, law, and current politics shape access. Joining creates duties and consequences. No faction offers membership, major quests, secrets, command, or special treatment simply because the player arrives.\n\nThe player affects faction politics through actions that reach relevant people. Factions also negotiate, fight, trade, recruit, fracture, and change leadership without player involvement. Relationships with faction members create political effects only where their position, conduct, visibility, and surrounding culture make those effects real.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "The Clans of the Sunmane": {
    "text": "The clan-confederation of the beastkin peoples of the Sunmane Steppe — dozens of clans (the Sunmane lions, the Painted-Claw hunters, the Kithwood foxes and birds, the Emberhunt wolves, and more) bound not by a ruler but by the great Moot, where the High Moot-Speaker keeps a peace made of hunt-right, guest-right, and hard-won compromise. The Clans field superb scouts, trackers, and pack-fighters, trade hides, amber, and rare beasts, and guard their homeland's borders with a wariness earned across centuries of being underestimated.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "The Delvers' Guild": {
    "text": "The independent guild that governs the Descent at Deepgate — separate from the surface Adventurers' Guild, with its own ranks, its own floor-clearances, and its own iron rules. The Delvers' Guild registers every diver, licenses parties by proven depth, taxes the spoils, runs the only sanctioned map of the upper floors, and maintains the safe-rests that keep the deep dive survivable. Its authority is absolute below the Rim: break Guild law in the labyrinth and you are simply not pulled out. It is a repeatable source of paying work — floor-clearances, retrieval, escort, and monster-cull contracts scaled to a delver's licensed depth.",
    "type": "worldLore",
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0
  },
  "aerfala-economy": {
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0,
    "text": "# Aerfála Economy\n\n## Currency\nAerfála uses Copper (cp), Silver (sp), and Gold (gp).\n- 1 sp = 100 cp\n- 1 gp = 100 sp = 10,000 cp\n\nCopper Coins, Silver Coins, and Gold Coins are separate inventory items. Quote the denomination normal for the transaction and convert coins explicitly when change is made.\n\n## Coin Flow\nTrack every actual payment, sale, wage, reward, fee, toll, theft, gift, exchange, and discovery. Remove coins only when they leave a character's possession. Add coins only when acquired. Do not charge routine costs that the scene skips completely, and do not award money that exists only in narration.\n\nTypical quest rewards:\n- F-rank: 50 cp to 3 sp\n- D-rank: 8 sp to 2 gp\n- B-rank: 5–15 gp\n- S-rank: 50–200 gp\n- SSS-rank: 200–1,000+ gp\n\n## Price Reference\n**Daily life:** 2 cp to 3 sp. Apple 2 cp; meal 10–30 cp; inn 10–80 cp.\n**Food and provisions:** 1 cp to 8 sp. Bread 2–10 cp; pig 1–3 sp; feast per head 50 cp to 3 sp.\n**Potions and alchemy:** 2–25 sp. Small 3–6 sp; standard 6–12 sp; large 15–25 sp.\n**Weapons:** 10 cp to 8 gp. Crude/F-rank 10–50 cp; iron/D-rank 1–5 sp; enchanted/A-rank 1–3 gp.\n**Armor:** 30 cp to 5 gp. Leather/D-rank 1–4 sp; chain/C-rank 4–10 sp; plate/A-rank 2–5 gp.\n**Accessories:** 2 sp to 3 gp. Minor charm 2–5 sp; rare 1–3 gp.\n**Tools and kits:** 50 cp to 2 gp. Adventurer's kit 1–2 sp; workshop set 5 sp to 2 gp.\n**Services:** 20 cp to 20 gp. Minor healing 5–15 sp; resurrection 5–20 gp; repair 50 cp to 8 sp.\n**Travel:** 10 cp to 100 gp. Ferry 10–50 cp; horse 3–8 sp; warhorse 1–5 gp; wyvern 20–100 gp.\n**Property:** 5 sp to 2,000 gp. Cottage 2–5 gp; townhouse 10–30 gp; castle 100–2,000 gp.\n\n## Income\n- Laborer: 20–50 cp/day.\n- Soldier: 1–8 sp/day.\n- Master craftsman: 5 sp to 2 gp/day.\n- Small merchant: 5 sp to 3 gp/month.\n- Great merchant: 5–50 gp/month.\n- Minor noble: 10–50 gp/month.\n\n## Price Formation\nLocation, supply, scarcity, urgency, legality, quality, condition, demand, season, transport, taxes, faction policy, reputation, relationships, and negotiation determine the final price. Frontier shortage raises prices; abundant supply lowers them. Discounts and exclusive stock follow an actual seller, institution, standing, agreement, or successful negotiation rather than a universal reward.\n\n## Special Rules\n- Slavery is legal in the Muirscáth Free Cities and frontier regions; it is illegal in Solasreach and Caelvir.\n- Legendary and mythic items have no ordinary market price. They enter play through discovery, inheritance, creation, theft, gift, award, exchange, conquest, or another concrete transfer.\n- The Demi-Plane Anchor is priceless and not an ordinary sale item.\n- Preserve the last confirmed coin totals and transactions. If a total is missing, reconstruct it from established records or leave it unresolved until verified; do not invent funds to favor or obstruct a purchase.",
    "type": "worldLore"
  },
  "demi-plane-regions": {
    "gameTick": 0,
    "lastUsedTick": 0,
    "text": "# Demi-Plane — Regions (Overview)\n\nThe Demi-Plane is a 5×5 grid of 25 region-squares, the pocket dimension created by Tsukiko Kuzunoha, the Mother Fox. Access: Demi-Plane Gate ability or Demi-Plane Anchor item only. Overviews are deliberately broad — discovery, settlement, and development fill them later.\n\n## Heartgate Basin (center)\nSacred fox-spirit garden-forest, the developed heart. The Gateway Clearing: gateway chamber (sole entry), Chamberlain's quarters (development planning), and the original clearing where every expansion begins. The Hearth Springs (recreation/commerce), Mana Heart (mana infrastructure/defense), and Verdant Plot (agriculture/settlement). The Chamberlain receives the creator here.\n\n## The Hidden Estate of the Mother Fox\nA standalone location whose true grounds nest within the Veiled Fox Sanctuary (sanctuary-local pin -6,-6) — Heian Shinden-zukuri estate and hidden clan seat of the fox-masked Kitsune no Kage. Wings: guardian front gate (opens only when Tsukiko summons it), central pavilion, sealed Kita-no-in, eastern rites hall, guest wing, moonlit southern dojo, crescent mirror lake garden. Outsiders perceive only a forgotten ruined temple. Attendants serve by devotion, not wages.\n\n## North Row (y = -12)\n- Still Frontier (NW, x:-2): cold highland spirit-forest — pale conifers, silver breath-mist, frost foxfire, slow spiraling snow. Cold-tolerant spirit-flora await discovery.\n- Pale Reaches (x:-1): misty white-stone highland badland — glowing silver fog valleys, lichen like constellations, fleeting ghost-landscapes. Largest reserve of potential.\n- North Field (x:0): boreal river-grass plain — shining channels that appear and vanish, ribbon clouds, upward glittering rain, luminous reed-seeds. Suited to farmland or a great hall.\n- The Fold (x:1): folded scrub-steppe — wave-terraces repeating in paper-folds; natural defense, disorienting but not dangerous.\n- Veiled Edge (NE, x:2): luminous boundary-scrub fading into shimmering fog-edge — aurora ground-glow. The boundary itself.\n\n## Mid-North Row (y = -11)\n- Veiled Fox Sanctuary (x:-2): Tsukiko's sacred private region — the merged temperate spirit-forest and fox-shrine realm. Bioluminescent fungi, foxfire motes, moss paths, and a dressing of temperate woodland give way to a mythic core: vermilion torii processions, shinboku heart, luminous bamboo spring, shimenawa paths, pale-violet foxfire lanterns. Entry ONLY by Tsukiko's will, the Demi-Plane Gate, or the Anchor. Its spirit-forest half fills the region's ecological role: repository of fungal and botanical resources, roots tapping the ambient mana flow.\n- First Meadow (x:-1): luminous wildflower meadow — foxfire-blooms, spiral pollen at dawn, the Beginning's Torii at its northwest edge. The dimension growing on its own.\n- Threshold March (x:0): open approach-grassland and reed-swaths — the natural northern route.\n- Stone Rise (x:1): rocky moss-highland — grey stone, lichen-terraces, mineral springs. The structural foundation.\n- Mirror Mere (x:2): twilight wetland — glowing pools holding reflections, shapeless lights beneath the surface.\n\n## Core Row (y = -10)\n- Quiet Shore (W, x:-2): pale coast — moon-white unmoving sand, soundless water-horizon, salt-grass chimes. Undecided between sea and land.\n- Westreach (x:-1): open mixed grassland — foxfire dew beads at dawn. Most practical terrain for construction.\n- Heartgate Basin (x:0) — the core (above).\n- Dawn Barrow (x:1): stream-fed river meadow — glowing water over red stone. The dimension's own hydrology.\n- Easthold (E, x:2): sunlit dry-grass upland — sparks of slow gold light. Richest energy: forges and workshops.\n\n## Mid-South Row (y = -9)\n- Low Reaches (x:-2): soft marshy lowland — will-o'-wisps, dream-shifting terrain. Stabilized only by intention.\n- Hollow Vale (x:-1): sheltered terraced vale — foxfire lanterns along terrace edges. Ideal for settlement.\n- Deep Expanse (x:0): vast prairie — humming tall grasses, sky and horizon everywhere. The largest blank canvas.\n- Southroll (x:1): golden hill country in perpetual late-afternoon — disciplined fireflies. The dimension's lifeboat.\n- Rift March (SE, x:2): broken marchland — dark clefts, blue seep-light, echo returning late. Potential dungeon ground.\n\n## South Row (y = -8)\n- Dim March (SW, x:-2): hushed moss-hollow — footsteps dimmed to nothing. The storage zone.\n- Grey Boundary (x:-1): fog-wall moor — flickering shapes in grey fog. The firmest edge; anchors the dimension in place.\n- Open Horizon (x:0): endless flat savanna — an ember-glow horizon; becomes exactly what is intended.\n- Fade (x:1): misting hill-fen — hills losing sound then color. The most permeable, unfinished boundary.\n- Last Edge (SE corner, x:2): still stony plain — no shadows, motionless air. The anchor point. Profound, not dangerous.\n\n## kitsune-no-kage — Estate Attendants\nThe Shadows of the Fox keep the estate: shrine-bound kitsune, distant descendants, orphaned fox spirits, yokai retainers, household spirits — some serving since before Jōgan. Fox-masked (white or black, marked in red) in sign of humility; bare-faced before the Lady is too intimate without permission. They speak only when addressed, never enter her sleeping sanctuary, and pass her veiled with a bow — unaware that the Still Flame is their own ancestral mother. When she unveils, every mask lowers and every flame bends toward her.",
    "type": "worldLore"
  }
}